Campaign Starting Before Group's Start Date:

If a Campaign's Start Date is before the Group's Start Date, the data for that Campaign should only be taken from the Group's Start Date onward.

For example: If Group X has a Start Date of January 10 and a Campaign X has a Start Date of January 1, the data for Campaign X within Group X should only be considered from January 10.

Display an Orange warning icon (
) after the Campaign’s name. Hovering over the icon should display the follow text “Start Date is out of range! Only data from {Group’s Start Date} will be taken!”.

The placeholder field should be replaced with the Group’s Start Date in the format of MonthName Day Year. For example: “Start Date is out of range! Only data from January 10 2024 will be taken!”.

Campaign Starting After Group's Start Date:

If a Campaign's Start Date is after the Group's Start Date, then the data should be taken starting from the Campaign's Start Date and continue until the earlier of the Campaign's End Date or the Group's End Date.

For example: If Group X has the date range of January 10 - 30 and a Campaign X has a date range of January 15 - 20, the data for Campaign X within Group X should only be considered from January 15 - 20. If Campaign Y has a date range of January 20 - February 5, the data for Campaign Y within Group X should only be considered from January 20 - 30.

Campaign Starting and Ending Within Group's Date Range:

If both the Campaign's Start Date and End Date fall within the Group's Start Date and End Date, then all data from the Campaign should be considered for that Group.

For example: If Group X runs from March 1 to March 31, and Campaign Y runs from March 5 to March 25, all data from Campaign Y should be included for Group X.

Campaign Ending After Group's End Date:

If a Campaign's End Date is after the Group's End Date, the data should be taken from the Campaign's Start Date to the Group's End Date.

For example: If Group X ends on April 30, but Campaign X ends on May 15, the data for Campaign X within Group X should only be considered up until April 30.

Display an Orange warning icon (
) after the Campaign’s name. Hovering over the icon should display the follow text “End Date is out of range! Only data until {Group’s End Date} will be taken!”.

The placeholder field should be replaced with the Group’s End Date in the format of MonthName Day Year. For example: “End Date is out of range! Only data until April 30 2024 will be taken!”.

Undefined Campaign End Date:

If a Campaign has an undefined End Date, it should be assumed that the Campaign continues indefinitely. The data should be taken from the Campaign's Start Date to the Group's End Date.

For example: If Group A runs from June 1 to June 30, and Campaign A starts on June 5 with an undefined end date, the data for Campaign A within Group A should be considered from June 5 to June 30.

Campaign Fully Out Of Range:

If a Campaign's date range does not overlap at all with a Group's date range, then no data from that Campaign should be considered for that Group.

For example: If Group B runs from July 1 to July 31, and Campaign B runs from August 1 to August 15, no data from Campaign B should be included for Group B.

The row of an out of range Campaign should be greyed out.

Display a  RED warning icon after the Campaign’s name. Hovering over the icon should display the follow text “This campaign is out of range!”.

Hovering over any other element in the row should change the cursor to not-allowed.

