That's a fair question, and it gets to the heart of how we model the relationship between operational execution (Groups) and strategic planning (Strategies). My reasoning for allowing a Many-to-Many (N:M) relationship, specifically enabling One Group to be associated with Many Strategies, stems from these points:

Real-World Resource Allocation: Often, a "Group" represents an operational unit, a channel focus, or a set of ongoing activities (e.g., "Paid Search Team & Campaigns," "Email Marketing Operations," "Organic Social Media Content"). These operational units are frequently leveraged to support multiple strategic initiatives simultaneously or sequentially, especially if those initiatives fall under the same broad Objective Category.Example: Your "Paid Search Group" (Category: Lead Generation) might run campaigns that contribute leads to both the "Q2 New Product Launch Strategy" and the "Ongoing Website Lead Generation Strategy" during the same period.

Both strategies aim for leads (same category), but have different specific KRs and contexts. Forcing the user to link the group to only one strategy would misrepresent how the resource is being used and make tracking its contribution to the other strategy impossible without workarounds (like duplicating the group).Flexibility Over Time: A long-running Group (e.g., "Always-On Brand Awareness Campaigns") might contribute to different, shorter-term Strategies over its lifespan. It could support the "Q1 Brand Strategy," then the "Q2 Brand Strategy," etc.

Associating the Group with each relevant Strategy allows for continuous tracking of its contribution to sequential plans.Avoiding Artificial Group Splitting: If we enforced a 1 Group -> 1 Strategy rule, users whose operational reality doesn't fit this neat box would be forced to create artificial duplicate groups. Using the Paid Search example above, they might create "Paid Search Group - Product Launch" and "Paid Search Group - Website Leads," even if it's the exact same team, budget pool (initially), and campaign structure executing both. This adds unnecessary administrative overhead for the user.

Focus on Objective Category Alignment: The primary guardrail is the Objective Category. A Group focused on "Conversions" can only link to Strategies also focused on "Conversions." This prevents illogical associations (e.g., linking a Conversion-focused group to a Brand Awareness strategy).

Allowing multiple strategy links within the same category acknowledges that multiple specific plans might exist under one broad objective, and operational units can contribute across them.Tracking Granularity: Allowing the N:M link enables more granular tracking. You can see precisely how "Group X" contributed to the KRs of "Strategy A" and how it contributed (perhaps differently, based on metric mapping) to the KRs of "Strategy B" during their respective (potentially overlapping) timeframes.

