Point 2: Fiscal Years vs. Strategy Duration

This is a classic tension!

Manager's View (Align Strategies to Fiscal Years): This often comes from a finance/reporting perspective. Budgets are typically allocated and reconciled annually (fiscally). Aligning strategies makes it much cleaner to track Strategy Spend against the Fiscal Year Budget it draws from. It simplifies reporting and performance reviews tied to fiscal periods.

Your View (Strategies Shouldn't Be Forced): This often comes from a marketing/operational perspective. Marketing initiatives, product launches, campaigns, etc., don't always respect calendar boundaries. A strategy might logically run from November to March. Forcing it into two separate strategies ("FY24 End" and "FY25 Start") can be artificial, disrupt continuity, and add overhead.

My Take & The System's Role:

The system should NOT force strategies to align with fiscal years. It should be flexible enough to support strategies that span fiscal year boundaries.

Why?

Model Reality: The tool should reflect how marketing often works, which isn't always tied to fiscal calendars. Forcing alignment creates awkward workarounds.

Validation Handles It: The budget validation logic we defined (Scenario 1 & 3, especially the pro-rating across periods) is specifically designed to handle this. When a strategy runs Nov-Mar:

The validation logic correctly calculates the available budget portion from the Nov & Dec CG budget (FY24).

It correctly calculates the available budget portion from the Jan, Feb, Mar CG budget (FY25).

It ensures the total Strategy Planned Budget doesn't exceed the sum of the available portions from both fiscal years' relevant CG budget allocations.

Separate Policy from Tool Constraint: Your manager can absolutely set an organizational policy that strategies should aim to align with fiscal years where practical. But the tool shouldn't make it impossible to create a strategically sound initiative that needs to cross that boundary. The tool provides the financial guardrails (via validation); policy guides the planning preference.

In summary: Build the tool to be flexible regarding strategy dates spanning fiscal years. Rely on the robust, period-aware budget validation to ensure financial compliance across those boundaries. Allow organizational policy, not system limitations, to guide the preferred alignment with fiscal periods.

