Address Terminology & Custom Objective Category (Crucial Fix):

Recommended Solution: Modify Step 1. Keep the list as "Select Objective". When a user selects a predefined Objective (e.g., "Brand Awareness"), the system implicitly knows its category. Add a mandatory "Objective Category" dropdown that ONLY appears and becomes required IF the user selects "Create Custom Objective". This dropdown would contain the standard categories ("Brand Awareness", "Lead Generation", etc.). This explicitly links every Objective (predefined or custom) to a Category needed for later association.

Alternative (Less Recommended): Rename the Step 1 list items (e.g., "Strategic Focus Areas") and have the user always write a specific Objective statement and select its Category. More work for predefined cases.

Clarify the Role of the "Metric" Field in KRs:

Recommended Solution (Label Only): Treat the "Metric" field associated with the KR in this workflow primarily as a descriptive label. It tells the user what the KR is measuring conceptually.

UI/UX: Make it clear (e.g., tooltip) that this is a description and the actual data source connection will happen during the later "Tracking Setup" phase (the granular mapping process we discussed).

AI Prompt Refinement: Instruct the AI to suggest descriptive metric labels that are clear and commonly understood.

Benefit: Keeps this initial Objective/KR definition step relatively simple.

Downside: Puts more pressure on the usability of the later granular mapping step, where the user needs to connect the KR description to the actual data. The mapping UI must clearly show the KR Title and its descriptive Metric label for context.

